                <h1 class="content-title">HAM Developer Patterns, APIs &amp; <span class="gradient-text">Automation</span></h1>
                <p class="content-subtitle">
                    Production engineering guide for ServiceNow architects and developers: Scoped sn_hamp APIs, safe AssetAndCISynchronizer extensions, barcode transform scripts, and anti-pattern mitigations.
                </p>
            </div>

            <!-- 1. Scoped APIs & Synchronizer -->
            <section class="content-section" id="scoped-apis">
                <h2>1. Scoped APIs &amp; The Asset-CI Synchronizer</h2>
                <p>
                    Hardware Asset Management Professional operates in the <code>sn_hamp</code> application scope. It exposes specialized script includes to manage lifecycle transitions, stock checks, and inventory reservations without hardcoding direct SQL updates.
                </p>

                <div class="grid-2" style="margin-top: 20px;">
                    <div class="card-basic">
                        <h4>AssetAndCISynchronizer Mechanics</h4>
                        <p>
                            Located in the global scope, <code>AssetAndCISynchronizer</code> manages state crosswalks between <code>alm_asset</code> and <code>cmdb_ci</code>:
                        </p>
                        <ul class="feature-bullet-list">
                            <li><strong>Recursive Guard:</strong> Uses internal transaction flags (<code>sys_synchronizing</code>) to ensure an update made to a CI does not re-trigger the asset update rule, preventing infinite loop recursion.</li>
                            <li><strong>Safe Extension Rule:</strong> Never modify the baseline <code>AssetAndCISynchronizer</code> directly. Instead, create an override script include or use the CSDM 4.0 Life Cycle Mapping table (<code>life_cycle_mapping</code>) to define custom state behaviors.</li>
                        </ul>
                    </div>

                    <div class="card-basic">
                        <h4>Scoped API: sn_hamp.HardwareAssetManager</h4>
                        <p>
                            Provides high-level programmatic interfaces for inventory automation:
                        </p>
                        <ul class="feature-bullet-list">
                            <li><code>getAvailableStock(modelId, stockroomId)</code>: Returns unreserved available quantity.</li>
                            <li><code>reserveAsset(assetId, ritmId)</code>: Safely shifts an asset from Available to Reserved.</li>
                            <li><code>createTransferOrder(fromStock, toStock, modelList)</code>: Generates validated transfer orders with child lines.</li>
                        </ul>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- 2. Production Script Cookbooks -->
            <section class="content-section" id="code-cookbooks">
                <h2>2. Production Script Cookbooks</h2>
                <p>
                    Below are battle-tested scripts designed for enterprise production environments:
                </p>

                <!-- Script 1: Barcode Sanitizer Transform -->
                <div style="margin-top: 28px;">
                    <h3>Cookbook 1: Barcode &amp; Serial Number Sanitizer Transform Script</h3>
                    <p>
                        Manufacturer 2D barcodes often include data identifiers (e.g. Dell appends <code>"1S"</code>, Apple uses <code>"S"</code> prefix for serial numbers). Ingesting raw strings creates duplicate hardware records. Use this transform script in your Import Set Table to sanitize strings:
                    </p>

                    <div class="code-block">
                        <div class="code-header">
                            <span class="code-lang">JavaScript (Import Set Transform Map)</span>
                        </div>
                        <div class="code-body">
<pre><code>(function runTransformScript(source, map, log, target /*undefined onStart*/ ) {
    var rawSerial = source.u_serial_number ? source.u_serial_number.toString().trim() : '';
    if (!rawSerial) return;

    // 1. Strip common OEM barcode application identifier prefixes
    // Examples: '1S102482390' -> '102482390' or 'S/N: ABC1234' -> 'ABC1234'
    var cleanSerial = rawSerial.replace(/^(1S|S|SN|S\/N:)\s*/i, '');

    // 2. Remove non-printable control characters and trailing whitespace
    cleanSerial = cleanSerial.replace(/[\x00-\x1F\x7F]/g, '').trim().toUpperCase();

    // 3. Query existing Hardware Asset to prevent duplicate inserts
    var grAsset = new GlideRecord('alm_hardware');
    grAsset.addQuery('serial_number', cleanSerial);
    grAsset.query();

    if (grAsset.next()) {
        log.info('HAM Transform: Existing asset found with serial ' + cleanSerial + ' -> Updating Asset ' + grAsset.getValue('asset_tag'));
        target.sys_id = grAsset.getValue('sys_id');
    } else {
        target.serial_number = cleanSerial;
    }
})(source, map, log, target);</code></pre>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <!-- Script 2: Programmatic HADO Creator -->
                <div style="margin-top: 36px;">
                    <h3>Cookbook 2: Automated Disposal Order (HADO) Generator via API</h3>
                    <p>
                        This server-side script identifies all assets in a stockroom that have been marked <code>Pending Disposal</code> for more than 14 days and packages them into a new <code>sn_hamp_disposal_order</code>:
                    </p>

                    <div class="code-block">
                        <div class="code-header">
                            <span class="code-lang">JavaScript (Script Include / Scheduled Script)</span>
                        </div>
                        <div class="code-body">
<pre><code>var HamDisposalAutomation = Class.create();
HamDisposalAutomation.prototype = {
    initialize: function() {},

    createBatchDisposalOrder: function(stockroomId, vendorCompanyId) {
        if (!stockroomId || !vendorCompanyId) {
            gs.error('HamDisposalAutomation: Missing required stockroom or vendor ID.');
            return null;
        }

        // 1. Find assets staged for disposal
        var assetIds = [];
        var grAsset = new GlideRecord('alm_hardware');
        grAsset.addQuery('stockroom', stockroomId);
        grAsset.addQuery('install_status', '6'); // In Stock
        grAsset.addQuery('substatus', 'pending_disposal'); // Pending Disposal
        grAsset.query();

        while (grAsset.next()) {
            assetIds.push(grAsset.getUniqueValue());
        }

        if (assetIds.length === 0) {
            gs.info('HamDisposalAutomation: No assets pending disposal in stockroom ' + stockroomId);
            return null;
        }

        // 2. Create the Hardware Asset Disposal Order header
        var grHado = new GlideRecord('sn_hamp_disposal_order');
        grHado.initialize();
        grHado.setValue('stockroom', stockroomId);
        grHado.setValue('vendor', vendorCompanyId);
        grHado.setValue('stage', 'verify'); // Stage 1
        grHado.setValue('short_description', 'Automated Monthly E-Waste Batch Pickup (' + assetIds.length + ' units)');
        var hadoId = grHado.insert();

        // 3. Link assets to the disposal order
        for (var i = 0; i < assetIds.length; i++) {
            var grLine = new GlideRecord('sn_hamp_m2m_asset_disposal_order');
            grLine.initialize();
            grLine.setValue('disposal_order', hadoId);
            grLine.setValue('asset', assetIds[i]);
            grLine.insert();
        }

        gs.info('HamDisposalAutomation: Created Disposal Order ' + grHado.getValue('number') + ' with ' + assetIds.length + ' assets.');
        return hadoId;
    },

    type: 'HamDisposalAutomation'
};</code></pre>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <!-- Script 3: Asset Depreciation Calculator -->
                <div style="margin-top: 36px;">
                    <h3>Cookbook 3: Straight-Line Depreciation Engine</h3>
                    <p>
                        Calculates accumulated monthly depreciation and current Net Book Value (NBV) for accounting audits:
                    </p>

                    <div class="code-block">
                        <div class="code-header">
                            <span class="code-lang">JavaScript (Financial Depreciation Calculator)</span>
                        </div>
                        <div class="code-body">
<pre><code>function calculateStraightLineDepreciation(assetSysId) {
    var grAsset = new GlideRecord('alm_hardware');
    if (!grAsset.get(assetSysId)) return null;

    var cost = parseFloat(grAsset.getValue('cost')) || 0;
    var residualValue = parseFloat(grAsset.getValue('salvage_value')) || 0;
    var purchaseDateStr = grAsset.getValue('purchase_date');

    if (!purchaseDateStr || cost <= 0) return null;

    var usefulLifeMonths = 36; // Default standard 3-year term
    var modelGr = grAsset.model.getRefRecord();
    if (modelGr.isValidRecord() && modelGr.useful_life > 0) {
        usefulLifeMonths = parseInt(modelGr.getValue('useful_life'), 10);
    }

    var purchaseDate = new GlideDateTime(purchaseDateStr);
    var now = new GlideDateTime();
    var diffMonths = (now.getYearLocalTime() - purchaseDate.getYearLocalTime()) * 12 + 
                     (now.getMonthLocalTime() - purchaseDate.getMonthLocalTime());
    
    diffMonths = Math.max(0, Math.min(diffMonths, usefulLifeMonths));

    // Straight line formula: (Cost - Salvage) / UsefulLife
    var depreciableBase = Math.max(0, cost - residualValue);
    var monthlyDepreciation = depreciableBase / usefulLifeMonths;
    var accumulatedDepreciation = monthlyDepreciation * diffMonths;
    var netBookValue = Math.max(residualValue, cost - accumulatedDepreciation);

    return {
        originalCost: cost.toFixed(2),
        usefulLifeMonths: usefulLifeMonths,
        monthsDepreciated: diffMonths,
        monthlyRate: monthlyDepreciation.toFixed(2),
        accumulatedDepreciation: accumulatedDepreciation.toFixed(2),
        netBookValue: netBookValue.toFixed(2)
    };
}</code></pre>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- 3. Flow Designer Subflows -->
            <section class="content-section" id="flow-designer">
                <h2>3. Flow Designer Subflows &amp; Decision Tables</h2>
                <p>
                    Modern HAM Pro processes (such as Asset Refresh, Loaner Requests, and Disposal Workflows) are built entirely in <strong>Flow Designer</strong> rather than legacy Workflow Editor.
                </p>

                <div class="info-card info-card--important">
                    <div class="info-card-title">Critical Architecture Rule: Sourcing Variable Integrity</div>
                    <p>
                        In the out-of-the-box flow <code>Hardware Asset Refresh Flow</code>, ServiceNow utilizes a system action named <strong>Update Sourcing set Variable</strong>. 
                        <strong>Never remove, rename, or reorder this step.</strong> Doing so breaks the link between the parent <code>sc_request</code> and the downstream automated sourcing task generation, causing requests to stall in the Approved state without generating catalog tasks.
                    </p>
                </div>

                <div class="grid-2" style="margin-top: 20px;">
                    <div class="card-basic">
                        <h4>Decision Tables for Dynamic Stock Routing</h4>
                        <p>
                            Instead of hardcoding stockroom assignment logic in script, use <strong>Decision Tables</strong> (<code>sys_decision</code>). Configure multi-parameter decision matrices:
                        </p>
                        <ul class="feature-bullet-list">
                            <li>Inputs: Requester Country, Device Model Category, Executive VIP Flag.</li>
                            <li>Outputs: Target Fulfillment Stockroom, Shipping Carrier Service Level (Next Day Air vs Ground), Asset Tag Prefix.</li>
                        </ul>
                    </div>

                    <div class="card-basic">
                        <h4>Subflow Modularization</h4>
                        <p>
                            Break complex workflows into reusable subflows:
                        </p>
                        <ul class="feature-bullet-list">
                            <li><code>HAM - Send Carrier Shipping Notification</code></li>
                            <li><code>HAM - Create Loaner Staging Task</code></li>
                            <li><code>HAM - Revoke User Software Licenses on Disposal</code></li>
                        </ul>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- 4. Critical Scheduled Jobs -->
            <section class="content-section" id="scheduled-jobs">
                <h2>4. Critical Scheduled Jobs in HAM Pro</h2>
                <p>
                    Several automated scheduled jobs govern background processing in the HAM module. Understanding their schedules is essential for debugging and batch performance tuning:
                </p>

                <div class="data-table-wrapper" style="margin-top: 16px;">
                    <table class="data-table">
                        <thead>
                            <tr>
                                <th>Scheduled Job Name</th>
                                <th>Execution Frequency</th>
                                <th>Target Table</th>
                                <th>Architectural Responsibility</th>
                            </tr>
                        </thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>HAM - Normalize Models</strong></td>
                                <td>Daily (Nightly)</td>
                                <td><code>cmdb_hardware_product_model</code></td>
                                <td>Pulls Content Delivery Service updates, normalizes models, and updates milestone dates (EOM, EOL, EOS, EOES).</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>HAM - Evaluate Stock Rules</strong></td>
                                <td>Daily (Early Morning)</td>
                                <td><code>alm_stock_rule</code></td>
                                <td>Calculates on-hand stock vs thresholds and automatically generates Purchase Orders or Transfer Orders.</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>SAM - Calculate Asset Refresh Eligibility</strong></td>
                                <td>Weekly</td>
                                <td><code>alm_hardware</code></td>
                                <td>Compares asset purchase dates against model useful life and sets <code>eligible_for_refresh = true</code>.</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>HAM - Loaner Asset Notifications</strong></td>
                                <td>Hourly</td>
                                <td><code>sn_hamp_loaner_asset_order</code></td>
                                <td>Checks upcoming reservations; fires Prepare tasks (T-2 days) and Reclaim tasks (T-1 day).</td>
                            </tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- 5. Pitfalls & Troubleshooting -->
            <section class="content-section" id="common-pitfalls">
                <h2>5. Common Pitfalls &amp; Troubleshooting Guide</h2>

                <div class="grid-2" style="margin-top: 20px;">
                    <div class="card-basic">
                        <div style="color: #ef4444; font-weight: 700; margin-bottom: 6px;">Anti-Pattern 1: The Dual-Write Infinite Loop</div>
                        <p>
                            <strong>Symptom:</strong> Modifying a field on <code>alm_hardware</code> via custom script causes the transaction to freeze or throw "Maximum stack depth exceeded".
                        </p>
                        <p>
                            <strong>Root Cause:</strong> A custom Business Rule on <code>alm_hardware</code> updates <code>cmdb_ci</code>, which triggers a rule on <code>cmdb_ci</code> that updates <code>alm_hardware</code> again.
                        </p>
                        <p>
                            <strong>Fix:</strong> Always wrap custom updates in <code>gr.setWorkflow(false);</code> or check <code>if (current.isActionAborted() || current.sys_synchronizing == true) return;</code>.
                        </p>
                    </div>

                    <div class="card-basic">
                        <div style="color: #ef4444; font-weight: 700; margin-bottom: 6px;">Anti-Pattern 2: Unmapped Model Categories</div>
                        <p>
                            <strong>Symptom:</strong> Discovery creates thousands of CIs, but no financial Asset records are generated.
                        </p>
                        <p>
                            <strong>Root Cause:</strong> The hardware product model has a Model Category where <code>asset_class</code> was left blank.
                        </p>
                        <p>
                            <strong>Fix:</strong> Ensure every hardware model category links <code>alm_hardware</code> to its corresponding <code>cmdb_ci_*</code> class. Run the OOTB diagnostic job <em>Create Assets for CIs without Assets</em>.
                        </p>
                    </div>

                    <div class="card-basic">
                        <div style="color: #ef4444; font-weight: 700; margin-bottom: 6px;">Anti-Pattern 3: Disposal Cancellation Past Transit</div>
                        <p>
                            <strong>Symptom:</strong> Technicians attempt to cancel an <code>sn_hamp_disposal_order</code> after the vendor pickup has departed.
                        </p>
                        <p>
                            <strong>Root Cause:</strong> By regulatory design, once the <code>Asset Departure</code> task is closed, ServiceNow permanently locks the order against cancellation to prevent chain of custody fraud.
                        </p>
                        <p>
                            <strong>Fix:</strong> If an asset was put on the wrong truck, document it via a security incident exception; do not attempt to bypass business rules via SQL.
                        </p>
                    </div>

                    <div class="card-basic">
                        <div style="color: #ef4444; font-weight: 700; margin-bottom: 6px;">Anti-Pattern 4: Negative Stockroom Balances</div>
                        <p>
                            <strong>Symptom:</strong> Consumable records (<code>alm_consumable</code>) show quantities of -5 or -12.
                        </p>
                        <p>
                            <strong>Root Cause:</strong> Technicians consume keyboards/cables via catalog tasks without first receiving incoming stock shipments into ServiceNow.
                        </p>
                        <p>
                            <strong>Fix:</strong> Enforce strict receiving dock scanning policies and configure stock rule alerts to lock out negative allocations.
                        </p>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </section>

